<h2>The post-award clock</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Award delivered</strong><p>The three-month window (extendable by thirty days) for a Section 34 challenge begins on receipt of the award.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Challenge and stay</strong><p>If the State entity files under Section 34, it must separately seek stay under Section 36(2); money-award stays ordinarily come with deposit or security under Section 36(3).</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Deposit and withdrawal</strong><p>Courts frequently permit the award-holder to withdraw the deposited amount against security, converting paper victory into cash flow pending the challenge.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Execution</strong><p>Absent a stay, execution proceeds under Section 36(1) as for a decree — with the CPC's government-specific steps folded in where the judgment-debtor is the State.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The strategic consequence of this architecture: the award-holder should be present and assertive at the stay hearing, not waiting passively for the challenge to conclude. The deposit order made there — its percentage, its form (cash versus bank guarantee), and the liberty to withdraw — determines the economics of the next two years far more than the eventual Section 34 judgment.</p>

<h2>Deposit practice: the real battlefield</h2>
<p>Three recurring contests shape stay-and-deposit hearings against State entities. First, the plea of safety — the argument that the government can always pay, so no deposit is needed. The statutory text points the other way: Section 36(3) directs regard to the CPC's money-decree stay principles, which are premised on security, and the proviso added in 2021 reserves unconditional stay for cases of prima facie fraud or corruption in the award or contract, not for solvency. Second, the form of security: departments prefer bank guarantees that cost budget lines nothing; award-holders should press for cash deposit with liberty to withdraw against security, which shifts the time-value of money. Third, interest discipline: post-award interest continues to run on unpaid amounts, a point worth quantifying at every hearing, because it converts delay from a costless strategy into an auditable liability for the paying officer.</p>

<h2>Executing against departments versus PSUs</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Government department</h3><p>Execution follows the CPC's state-decree procedure — a statutory interval before process issues, payment through budgetary sanction, and protections for sovereign property. Pressure points: time-bound directions, interest accrual, and accountability of the disbursing office.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Public sector undertaking</h3><p>A separate juristic entity whose bank accounts and receivables are ordinarily attachable like any company's. Pressure points: garnishee attachment of accounts and of amounts payable to the PSU by its own debtors.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practical sequencing for the award-holder</h2>
<p>Serve the award and a payment demand immediately, with a computation of principal and running interest — the file that eventually moves inside a department is the one with arithmetic on it. Calendar the challenge window, and prepare the execution petition to be filed the day it lapses unexercised. Identify the paying unit and its banker early: execution petitions that name attachable accounts and receivables move; petitions that name only the entity wait. Where the award arises from an MSME transaction, invoke the Section 19 MSMED pre-deposit against any challenge. And throughout, keep settlement space open — departments that will not pay an award will often pay a joint-satisfaction figure blessed by the execution court, because it closes the audit question that unofficial delay keeps alive.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on the enforcement of domestic arbitral awards against government departments and public sector entities under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 and the CPC. It is informational only; execution strategy in any matter depends on the award, the entity and the assets involved.</p>
</div>
